# terraform/authentik

Single sign-on objects as code: OIDC/proxy/SAML providers, applications, groups,
and the policy bindings that gate them. The shape and its security defaults come
from `weisssrv-lib//terraform/modules/authentik-sso` at the ref pinned in
`main.tf`; the inventory is `sso.tf`.

The authentik *deployment* is Flux's (`kubernetes/apps/authentik`). This module
only configures the objects inside it, so the server must be up and reachable at
`https://auth.{{ internal_domain }}` before the first plan.

## Running it

```bash
task terraform:authentik-init
task terraform:authentik-import   # only when adopting or rebuilding state
task terraform:authentik-plan     # review object by object
task terraform:authentik-apply    # refuses -auto-approve on purpose
```

An SSO misconfiguration locks every user out of every application at once. Keep
a break-glass path available — a local admin account, or one route that bypasses
SSO — until the post-apply login checks pass, and log in to at least one
application before you walk away.

## Adding an application

1. Add the provider to the right map in `sso.tf` (`oauth2_providers` for OIDC,
   `proxy_providers` for forward-auth, `saml_providers` for SAML).
2. If the application keeps its own copy of the client secret, add a variable
   for it in `variables.tf`, add the entry to `oauth2_client_secrets`, and add
   the matching `TF_VAR_…` line to the `terraform:authentik-*` tasks pointing at
   the SAME secret-store item the application reads. Without an entry, authentik
   generates a secret that only exists in Terraform state and the app can never
   learn it.
3. Add the `applications` entry, a group, and a `policy_bindings` entry. **An
   application with no binding is open to every authenticated user.**

## Renaming and removing

Applications, all three provider kinds, groups, the custom scope mappings and
the embedded outpost carry `lifecycle { prevent_destroy = true }` in the
library module — destroying any of them is a live outage (a slug *is* the OIDC
issuer path; a group destroy takes its memberships and every binding pointing at
it). Policy bindings are exempt.

The guard is module-side, so it cannot be switched off from here:

```bash
terraform state rm 'module.sso.authentik_application.this["retired-app"]'
# then delete the sso.tf entry, and delete the object in authentik itself
```

Renaming a map key is the same operation in disguise — add a `moved {}` block
instead, which `prevent_destroy` does not block.

An application that no `policy_bindings` entry names fails the plan rather than
quietly becoming reachable by every authenticated user; a tile that really is
open to everyone declares `allow_unbound = true`.

## Adoption and recovery

Objects created in the authentik UI are brought under management with `import`
blocks in `imports.tf`, then `terraform plan` until it is clean. The module's
resource addresses are stable and keyed by their `sso.tf` map key:

```hcl
import {
  to = module.sso.authentik_application.this["grafana"]
  id = "grafana"                          # the slug
}

import {
  to = module.sso.authentik_provider_oauth2.this["grafana"]
  id = "12"                               # the provider pk
}

import {
  to = module.sso.authentik_group.this["grafana-users"]
  id = "018f0000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"   # the group uuid
}

import {
  to = module.sso.authentik_policy_binding.this["grafana"]
  id = "018f0000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"   # the binding uuid
}
```

Delete a block once its object is in state — a kept block is harmless but noisy.

**Losing this state is not a re-apply.** Group names are not unique server-side
while application slugs are, so an apply against a live server with empty state
silently duplicates every group and then hard-fails on the applications. The
recovery path is re-import, which only works if the address↔id table is current:

```bash
task terraform:authentik-import   # runs ./import.sh: imports, skips what is in state
task terraform:authentik-plan     # must be clean before any apply
```

Keep `import.sh` complete. After every apply that creates objects, read the ids
back out (`terraform output application_ids policy_binding_ids group_ids`) and
update the table — a list written once at adoption time is not a recovery path.

## Custom scope mappings

`oauth2_providers[*].scope_mappings` (and the module-wide
`oauth2_scope_mappings`) hold two kinds of entry in one ordered list: a
*managed* id, read from the blueprint-provided mappings, or `custom:<key>`
naming an entry of `custom_scope_mappings` in `sso.tf`, which this module
authors.

That is the escape hatch for an application that refuses a login whose ID token
lacks a claim authentik does not emit by default — the stock `email` scope
hardcodes `email_verified: false`, and some applications reject it. Give the
replacement the same `scope_name` as the built-in it stands in for and leave the
built-in out of that provider's list; providers that do not name it keep the
stock mapping. Order is what the API stores, so the list is state, not style.

Do not assert a claim the account model does not justify — an `expression` runs
server-side on every token issue and is readable by any authentik admin.

## Security defaults worth knowing

- **Grant types** default to `authorization_code` + `refresh_token`. `password`
  (ROPC) bypasses the authentication flow's stages *including MFA*, and
  `implicit`/`hybrid` return tokens in the URL fragment. Importing an existing
  deployment usually pulls in all seven, because that is what the admin UI
  creates — tighten them in a supervised apply with a login test per app.
- **Redirect URIs** default to `strict` matching. authentik evaluates `regex`
  mode with `re.fullmatch`, where `.` matches any character, so the module
  rejects a regex URI with an unescaped dot: `https://app\.example\.com/cb`.
- **Consent**: the default authorization flow never prompts a signed-in user.
  Set `authorization_flow_slug` to the explicit-consent flow if you want a
  first-use prompt per application.

## Credentials

| Item | Field | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| `Authentik Terraform Token` | `credential` | The authentik API |
| `<App> SSO` | client secret field | One per OIDC application |
| `Git Terraform State Token` | `credential` | The GitLab state backend |

## Changing SSO provider

`kubernetes/apps/authentik` plus this module is the whole SSO backend. A
replacement has to provide: an OIDC issuer the apps can point at, a forward-auth
middleware Traefik can call for apps without OIDC, and a group model the
bindings can express. The template's [ARCHITECTURE.md](https://git.ericsweiss.com/eric/weisssrv-cluster-template/-/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md)
records the seam.
